{
  "0": "Add a fixed training-frequency input feature: count item occurrences only in each category's training split and scale input item embeddings by 0.75 + 0.5*log1p(count)/log1p(max_count), keeping padding zero. Persist these constants as checkpoint buffers. This bounded signal may help gated attention distinguish common anchors from rare items without adding parameters or a separate scorer. Keep candidate embeddings, embedding design, HSTU topology, positions, readout, loss and optimizer unchanged.",
  "1": "Replace absolute padded-column position IDs with cumulative nonpadding position IDs, leaving padding at zero. Short histories then share position representations according to observed sequence order, potentially improving transfer across the predominantly short histories. Retain history truncation, causal gated HSTU attention and final-real-position readout. Apply the identical position rule during training and prediction; leave features, embedding tables, scoring, loss and optimizer unchanged.",
  "2": "Replace pairwise softplus loss with equal-weight positive and negative binary logistic losses: softplus(-positive_score) + softplus(negative_score), averaged over valid target positions. Unlike a loss depending only on score differences, this also constrains absolute logits and may reduce score drift with tied item embeddings. Keep the existing sampled pairs, masking, HSTU model, readout, optimizer, scheduler and training schedule unchanged.",
  "3": "Use unit-L2-normalized nonpadding item embeddings consistently for both sequence inputs and candidate lookup, retaining the existing sqrt(hidden_units) input multiplier and dot-product scoring. Keep padding exactly zero and retain tied embeddings and dimension 64. This may reduce frequency-driven embedding-norm disparities and improve directional discrimination. Change only embedding normalization, with identical behavior in training and the generated predictor; preserve HSTU topology, history policy, loss and optimizer."
}
